---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';

const site = siteFromHost();
const path = '/compare/omnisend-vs-postscript';
const title = 'Omnisend vs Postscript for Shopify: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = "Compare Omnisend and Postscript for Shopify with current email rollout scope, SMS reply ownership, cross-channel suppression, and quote requirements.";

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can Postscript replace Omnisend email?",
    "answer": "Its current listing advertises email rolling out in phases. Confirm access and demonstrate the required email journeys before treating it as a replacement."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I use Omnisend and Postscript together?",
    "answer": "A split design needs verified event exchange, channel ownership, suppression and reply handling. A shared Shopify connection alone does not prove that the systems coordinate queued messages."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does SMS revenue share decide which tool to choose?",
    "answer": "No fixed percentage is a reliable decision rule. Evaluate required messaging features, staffing, account eligibility, total cost and a documented incremental-value experiment."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-[#5d3a1d]">/</span>
        <span class="text-[#5d3a1d]">Omnisend vs Postscript</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="editorial-kicker">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Omnisend vs Postscript for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">
          Omnisend versus Postscript is a choice about channel ownership and account scope. A coordinated program may use one supplier or a split stack, but the decision must reflect what the account can actually run today, including Postscript’s phased email rollout.
        </p>
      </header>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <p><em>Scope checked November 24, 2026. Supplier descriptions support product scope only; the proposed exercises below are not completed tests or observed merchant results.</em></p>
        <h2>Current scope changes the old comparison</h2>
        <p>The <a class="link" href="https://apps.shopify.com/postscript-sms-marketing">Postscript listing</a> describes SMS, RCS and email, explicitly stating that email is rolling out in phases. The <a class="link" href="https://apps.shopify.com/omnisend">Omnisend listing</a> advertises email, SMS and web push. It is therefore inaccurate to call Postscript categorically SMS-only, or to assume every existing Postscript account can immediately replace an email platform. Obtain the account-specific scope first.</p>
        <h2>Design the ownership map</h2>
        <p>Assign one owner for welcome email, promotional email, cart email, SMS cart, product-drop texts, push and winback. For each, name the sending system, permission source, purchase-exclusion event and emergency pause owner. A split design should state which system has authority when events arrive out of order. Do not enable both SMS cart workflows while waiting to decide. A combined invoice or a shared customer record is not evidence of coordinated frequency control.</p>
        <h2>Proposed rehearsal: purchase between email and SMS</h2>
        <p>This is an unperformed scenario. A test shopper enters recovery, receives an eligible email, purchases, and then replies to a previously sent text. Inspect the order timestamp, event arrival, cancellation of pending promotions and reply routing. Repeat with a delayed event and an opt-out. Specify the expected result before configuring either product. If an integration cannot cancel a queued message, redesign the sequence or retain one recovery owner rather than relying only on longer delays.</p>
        <h2>Replies require a staffed workflow</h2>
        <p>For product questions, decide whether automation, support staff or the marketer handles a reply. Demonstrate escalation for an unresolved question, unavailable stock and a request to stop messages. Check conversation visibility and who can pause promotions while support is active. Supplier descriptions of conversational tools do not establish answer accuracy, safe routing or staffing coverage. Measure the real backlog and resolution effort in an authorized pilot rather than assigning either supplier a blanket conversation advantage.</p>
        <h2>Permission and sender continuity are separate work</h2>
        <p>Record the permission evidence for each channel and signup source. Do not turn email subscribers into SMS recipients merely because both fields exist on a profile. Before moving texting, obtain the receiving supplier’s account-specific import, sender and number-transfer requirements; do not cancel the previous service based on a generic overlap timetable. Verify opt-outs and settings with the responsible operator. This is an operational checklist, not a legal compliance certification.</p>
        <h2>Welcome, post-purchase and winback still matter</h2>
        <p>If consolidating, demonstrate the full email program as well as promotional texts: signup-source welcome, cart purchase exclusion, gift-aware post-purchase and recency-based winback. If splitting, decide whether an email promotion should suppress an SMS offer and how that decision reaches the other system. Keep service notifications distinct from promotional triggers. A successful product-drop demonstration is insufficient evidence that every lifecycle dependency can move.</p>
        <h2>Model messaging cost without invented uplift</h2>
        <p>Request quotes for the same audience, destination mix, message format, expected segments per text, campaign calendar and peak demand. Include platform fees, usage, carrier or sender charges, support, integration work and any email rollout terms. Ask what is charged for the actual message types and destinations you intend to use. Compare incremental contribution after discounts and messaging expense in a later controlled experiment; do not sum two platforms’ attributed revenue as though both independently caused each sale.</p>
        <h2>Consolidate only after dependencies are proved</h2>
        <p>A unified Omnisend design is worth considering if it meets the required channel sequence and simplifies ownership. A Postscript-led or split design is worth considering if demonstrated capabilities justify its staffing and coordination costs. Confirm phased email eligibility before removing the existing email sender. Preserve exports and suppression, pause the replaced triggers, and retain a rollback plan. No revenue-share threshold or supplier ranking replaces these account-specific decisions.</p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-postscript">Klaviyo vs Postscript</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/postscript-vs-attentive">Postscript vs Attentive</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-privy">Omnisend vs Privy</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/beauty">Email apps for beauty stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Omnisend vs Postscript FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
